From: ThirdReality <dev@3reality.com>
Subject: [PATCH] fix(wifi): don't free a listed scan entry when RSN parsing fails

wifi_mac_scan_rx()'s error path corrupts the scan table and takes the box down
with a NULL dereference inside the scan list walk:

  Internal error: Oops: 0000000096000004 [#1] PREEMPT SMP
  pc : wifi_mac_scan_rx+0x59c/0x83c [vlsicomm]
  Call trace:
    wifi_mac_scan_rx        <- wifi_mac_recv_probersp <- wifi_mac_recv_mgmt
    <- wifi_mac_input       <- wifi_mac_input_all     <- wifi_mac_rx_complete
    <- drv_intr_rx_ok       <- hal_soft_rx_cs         <- hal_rx_thread

The function takes one of two entries: an existing one from the scan table
(oldse, already linked into st->st_hash[] and st->st_entry), or a freshly
kzalloc()ed one that is not linked anywhere yet.  The "goto fail" taken when
wifi_mac_parse_counterpart_rsn() rejects an AP's RSN IE then has to unlink the
entry only in the first case.  It gets that test wrong:

    if (WIFINET_ADDR_EQ(se, oldse)) {

WIFINET_ADDR_EQ() is memcmp(a1, a2, 6), meant for MAC addresses.  Applied to
two struct scaninfo_entry pointers it compares the first six bytes of the
structs instead of the pointers, and dereferences oldse - which is NULL on
every newly allocated entry.  So:

  - oldse == NULL: memcmp() reads through a NULL pointer.  Both operands are
    kmalloc'd from the same 128-byte-aligned bucket and the leading bytes are
    list_head pointers into the same slab, so the comparison is junk even when
    it does not fault outright.

  - oldse != NULL: if those six bytes happen to differ, the list_del_init()
    pair is skipped and the entry is kfree()d while still linked, leaving
    st_hash[hash] and st_entry pointing at freed memory.  The next walker -
    wifi_mac_recv_probersp()'s hash lookup, wifi_mac_scan_flush(), or
    wifi_mac_scan_rx() itself - follows the dangling se_hash into the freed
    slab.  That is the Oops above: faulting address 0, x0 holding the stale
    entry, and the faulting insn b9400300 loading from it.

Reproducer is simply an AP in range whose RSN IE this parser refuses (it only
accepts RSN_VERSION with recognised cipher/AKM selectors, and rejects WPA3
suites among others, all of which log "RSN akm cipher wrong" / "RSN bad
version" first) whose BSSID is already in the scan table.  Hence the crash at
~12.9s, right as NetworkManager kicks off the first scan.

Fix both halves:

  - compare the pointers (se == oldse), which is what the code meant;
  - free only an entry that was never published, so the table can never point
    at freed memory.  A listed oldse is left in place with its previous, still
    coherent contents: it was already there before this probe response arrived
    and the ageing path will retire it normally.

The unlink-then-free that the old code attempted was itself wrong for a listed
entry, because callers of wifi_mac_scan_rx() keep using the table right after
it returns.  Not touching a listed entry is both simpler and safe.  Dropping
the WIFI_SCAN_SE_LIST_LOCK() section with it is fine: the remaining work no
longer touches either list.

This path runs from hal_rx_thread under GFP_ATOMIC, so it must not sleep;
nothing here does.

Applies on top of jethome-0004.
---
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/w1/vmac/wifi_mac_scan.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/w1/vmac/wifi_mac_scan.c
@@ -821,17 +821,23 @@
 
     return;
 
 fail:
-     WIFI_SCAN_SE_LIST_LOCK(st);
-     if (WIFINET_ADDR_EQ(se, oldse)) {
-         list_del_init(&se->se_list);
-         list_del_init(&se->se_hash);
-         printk("[Micro]%s_%d,delete oldse\n", __func__, __LINE__);
-     }
-     WIFI_SCAN_SE_LIST_UNLOCK(st);
-    FREE(se,"sta_add.se");
-    printk("[Micro]%s_%d\n", __func__, __LINE__);
+    /*
+     * Only a never-published entry may be freed.  When se == oldse it is
+     * already linked into st_hash[]/st_entry and callers keep walking those
+     * lists after we return, so freeing it left the table pointing into freed
+     * slab memory.  Leave it linked with its previous contents instead;
+     * ageing retires it normally.
+     */
+    if (se != oldse) {
+        DPRINTF(AML_DEBUG_WARNING, "%s %d RSN parse failed, dropping new entry\n",
+            __func__, __LINE__);
+        FREE(se, "sta_add.se");
+    } else {
+        DPRINTF(AML_DEBUG_WARNING, "%s %d RSN parse failed, keeping listed entry\n",
+            __func__, __LINE__);
+    }
     return;
 }
 
 static void quiet_intf (struct wlan_net_vif *wnet_vif, unsigned char enable)
